 Here, I'll utilize the properties of complex numbers. Given that $|z| = 1$, we know that $z$ lies on the unit circle in the complex plane. This property allows us to express $z$ in the form $e^{i\theta}$ for some real $\theta$. Similarly, since $|w| = 3$, $w$ lies on a circle of radius 3. Let's consider $w = 3e^{i\phi}$ for some real $\phi$.

Given $|z+w| = 2$, we aim to find $\left | \frac{1}{z} + \frac{1}{w} \right|$. The key insight here is to manipulate the expression using the given conditions.

We have \begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{1}{z} + \frac{1}{w} & = & \frac{w}{wz} + \frac{z}{wz} \\
& = & \frac{3e^{i\phi} + e^{i\theta}}{3e^{i\phi}e^{i\theta}} \\
& = & \frac{3e^{i\phi} + e^{i\theta}}{3e^{i(\phi+\theta)}} \\
& = & \frac{3e^{i\phi} + e^{i\theta}}{3e^{i\phi}e^{i\theta}} \\
& = & \frac{3 + e^{-i(\phi+\theta)}}{3}
\end{eqnarray*}

Thus, \begin{eqnarray*}
\left | \frac{1}{z} + \frac{1}{w} \right| & = & \left | \frac{3 + e^{-i(\phi+\theta)}}{3} \right| \\
& = & \frac{1}{3} \left | 3 + e^{-i(\phi+\theta)} \right| \\
& = & \frac{1}{3} \left | 3 + e^{i(\phi+\theta)} \right| \\
& = & \frac{1}{3} \left | 2 + 5e^{i(\phi+\theta)} \right| \\
& = & \frac{1}{3} \sqrt{2^2 + 5^2} \\
& = & \frac{1}{3} \sqrt{29}
\end{eqnarray*}
Therefore, the answer is $\frac{1}{3} \sqrt{29}$. </answer>

